[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m identical stdout, identical (empty) stderr and exit status 0 from both programs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the C program branches on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The program has exactly one input-dependent decision:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int x = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m scanf("%d", &x);   // return value ignored
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m driver(x);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m return 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * On a successful conversion, `x` becomes the converted value truncated to `int`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * On a **matching failure** or **EOF**, `scanf` does not store anything, so `x`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   keeps its initializer `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver()` is unconditional and has no early returns: it zero-initializes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `house_t`, sets `floors`/`bedrooms`/`bathrooms`, and `print_hex` walks
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `sizeof(house)` == 16 bytes emitting `%02x` each, then one `"\n"`. There is no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error path, no `stderr` output and no non-zero exit anywhere in the program, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr is always empty and the status is always 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors that were verified rather than assumed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places where a naive translation would diverge. Each was checked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against the C binary; the Rust code already handled all of them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Behavior | Observed C result | Notes |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Struct object representation | `floors`@0, `bedrooms`@4, `bathrooms`@8, size 16, **no padding holes** | e.g. input `1` → `01000000030000000000000000000040`. The `= {0}` plus full field assignment makes the whole image defined; `2.0` is `0x4000000000000000` little-endian. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `%d` skips whitespace, including newlines | `"\n\n\n   42\n"` → 42 | `scanf` reads *across* lines; a `fgets`-style line read would have failed here. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `%d` stops at the first non-digit | `42abc`→42, `3.9`→3, `12 34`→12, `1e9`→1, `1,234`→1 | Base 10 only, so `0x10` → **0** (the `x` terminates the conversion), and `010` → **10**, not octal 8. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Sign handling | `+5`→5, `-0`→0 | A sign with no following digit (`-`, `+`, `--5`, `+-5`, `- 5`, `-a`) is a matching failure → `x` stays 0. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Failed conversion keeps the initializer | `abc`, `_5`, `.5`, `!!!`, `""`, `"   "` → 0 | The return value of `scanf` is ignored, so failure is silent and still prints the `floors == 0` image. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Truncation to `int` | `4294967296` (2^32) → 0; `4294967297` → 1; `2147483648` → INT_MIN bytes; `2147483647999` → `ffffffff` | glibc converts with a 64-bit `long` and stores through an `int *`; the low 32 bits survive. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Out-of-`long` saturation | `9223372036854775808` and `"9"×100000` → `ffffffff` (i.e. `LONG_MAX` truncated to −1); the negative forms → `00000000` (`LONG_MIN` truncated to 0) | glibc's `%d` goes through `strtol`, which clamps to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`. Formally UB in ISO C, but this is what the reference binary does, so the Rust code reproduces the clamp-then-truncate exactly. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Arbitrarily long digit runs | `"0"×100000 + "7"` → 7 | No width limit, so leading zeros never overflow. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Non-text stdin | `\x00`, `\xff\xfe`, `\x80\x819` handled without error | The Rust side treats stdin as raw bytes, so invalid UTF-8 cannot cause a panic or a decode error. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Unread stdin / closed stdin | identical output, exit 0, no hang | Only one conversion happens; the rest of the stream is discarded. Verified with `0<&-`, `/dev/null`, and 300 KB of trailing junk. |
